the carved artistry of its sculptors.. Location: The unmatched grandeur of the Ranakpur temple stands amid the Aravalli hills, in the heart of a remote and wondrous valley, encircled by forest like a garland around this enchanting landscape.. History: In the 15th century of the Vikram era, Acharya Shri Somsundarsuriji, a man of magnetic personality, came into contact with the merchant Dharnashah, in whose heart he kindled a powerful spiritual light. At the age of twenty-one, Dharnashah undertook the pilgrimage to Shatrunjay, the holiest of Jain pilgrimage sites. Dharnashah took a vow of lifelong celibacy together with his wife. Owing to his sharp intellect, deep administrative ability, and leadership qualities, he rose to the position of minister under King Rana Kumbha. One day Dharnashah felt a strong urge to build a temple to the first Tirthankara, Lord Shri Rishabhdev (Adinath), one that would have no equal in beauty among other temples. One night, in a dream, Dharnashah beheld the \\\"Nalini Gulm Vimana\\\" (a celestial abode in the twelfth Devalok), considered the most beautiful of the heavenly dwellings of the gods. Dharnashah resolved that the temple should be as beautiful as this heavenly abode. He therefore bought land from Rana Kumbha and founded a village named \\\"Ranpur,\\\" later called Ranakpur. Craftsmen were summoned from near and far and asked to build the temple according to the dream. A beautiful and magnificent three-storey, four-faced (chaturmukh) temple took shape, its carving unique and unmatched in the world. The foundation was laid in the year 1433 of the Vikram era. The entire temple rests on pillars, numbering 1,444 in all, each carved differently. The temple is built so that the Lord's face can be seen from any corner, and not a single pillar obstructs the view. This temple contains 84 images (symbolising liberation from the cycle of 8.4 million forms of rebirth). It is also known by names such as \\\"Dharan Vihar,\\\" \\\"Trilokya Deepak Prasad,\\\" \\\"Tribhuvan Vihar,\\\" \\\"Nalini Gulm Vimana,\\\" and \\\"Chaturmukh Prasad.\\\". Directly opposite the main temple, to the north, stands the temple of Lord Shri Ranakpura Parshwanath, built in the 15th century. It is said that while work was underway on the main temple, the craftsmen, in their spare time, built this temple from materials left over from the main construction, and installed in it the image of Parshwanath, which came to be known as \\\"Ranakpura Parshwanath.\\\" Every year a fair (mela) is held here on Kartik Purnima, Falgun Purnima, and Chaitra Purnima.. Other Temples: The main temple is that of Lord Adinath. There were 6 other temples here, but today only 3 remain — the other 2 belong to Lord Neminath and Lord Parshwanath, along with a Surya (Sun) temple and a temple of Chakreshwar Devi.. Art and Sculpture: The four-faced temple of Ranakpur is built on a high plateau, its three-storey roof of marble, on which the sculptor, through his supreme artistic genius, has carved a poem of devotion and spiritual grandeur in stone. Above the steps of the main entrance (the western gateway) is a magnificent carving of the leaves of the wish-fulfilling Kalpavriksha tree, which fulfils the wishes of pilgrims. On the right can be seen the image of the master mason Drepa (Deva), a native of Mandar, who was a yogi and lived in the temple in the garb of an ascetic; his descendants still live there today and carry out the temple's repair work. Inside the temple, on the left, a statue of Dharnashah has been carved into a pillar so that his name would not be openly advertised, while allowing him to always gaze upon the Lord. There stands a Rayan tree planted by Dharnashah 500 years ago, which still exists today; beneath it are images of the footprints (charan paduka) of Lord Rishabhdev, a reminder that he attained omniscience (kevalgyan) beneath a Rayan tree. At the centre, to the right of the temple, is a marble samavasarana of Lord Mahavir. The scene of the ordeal (upsarg) inflicted upon Lord Parshwanath by Kamath is depicted, with an artistic carving of a canopy of a thousand serpent hoods above the Lord's head, in which sixteen serpent-queens assist a serpent-king in protecting the Lord, their intertwined tails carved with great artistry — this carving is made from a single stone. A model of Shatrunjay has also been well designed here. The image of Lord Parshwanath is made of touchstone. There are two pairs of metal bells weighing 13 mann, whose sound carries up to three miles.. Directions: The nearest railway station, Falna, is 35 km away; the nearest town, Sadri, is 10 km away. Bus service and private vehicles are available. The nearest airport is Udaipur, 90 km away, and Jodhpur, 170 km away. There is also a large dharamshala and bhojanshala near the temple.. Scripture References: There is an inscription in the main Adinath temple dated V.S. 1496, attesting to the temple's antiquity. The temples of Ranakpur are mentioned in \\\"Ranipur Chaturmukh Prasad Stavan,\\\" \\\"Shri Shankheshwar Parshwanath Chhand,\\\" \\\"Tirthmala,\\\" \\\"108 Namgarbhit Shri Godiji Parshwanath Stavan,\\\" \\\"Shri Hirvijaysuri Ras,\\\" \\\"108 Namgarbhit Shri Parshwanath Stavan,\\\" \\\"135 Namgarbhit Shri Parshwanath Stavan,\\\" \\\"Ranakpur Tirth Stavan,\\\" and others. The history and grandeur of this temple are beautifully described in the \\\"Som Sobhagya Kavya.\\\". Hymn of Praise: In Ranakpur Dham, where Dharnashah brought splendour to life, the temple of Lord Parshwa was established together with all the shrines; here where the tirtha of Adinath stands, Lord Parshwa too remains ever awake. \\\"With loving devotion I bow in reverence to Shri Ranakpura Lord Parshwa.\\\". Chanting Mantra: \\\"Om Hreem Arham Shri Ranakpura Parshwanathaya Namah\\\". Trust: Shri Ranakpur Parshwanath Shwetambar Jain Tirth, Sheth Shri Anandji Kalyanji Pedhi, Post: Ranakpur – 306702, District: Pali, Station: Falna, State – Rajasthan, India. Phone: 02934-85021, Mobile: 9829833816.. (From here, having completed the pilgrimage of a total of 79 Lords Parshwanath, the journey continues to the remaining 7 Parshwanaths of Marwar — at Nadlai, Varkana, Pali, Mundwa, Kapardaji, and Medtaroad.)\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1b:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mb-8 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"a\\\",null,{\\\"href\\\":\\\"https://goo.gl/maps/1ePqrwBYa5vMvAgr9\\\",\\\"target\\\":\\\"_blank\\\",\\\"rel\\\":\\\"noopener noreferrer\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"inline-flex items-center gap-2 rounded-sm bg-maroon px-5 py-2.5 font-ui text-sm font-medium text-ivory hover:bg-maroon-deep transition-colors\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L1a\\\",null,{\\\"ref\\\":\\\"$undefined\\\",\\\"iconNode\\\":\\\"$8:2:props:children:props:children:1:props:children:0:props:iconNode\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"lucide-map-pin h-4 w-4\\\",\\\"aria-hidden\\\":true}],\\\"Open in Google Maps\\\"]}],null,[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L20\\\",null,{\\\"text\\\":\\\"$21\\\",\\\"lang\\\":\\\"en\\\",\\\"label\\\":\\\"Listen\\\",\\\"stopLabel\\\":\\\"Stop\\\"}]]}]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1c:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mb-10 grid grid-cols-2 gap-2 sm:grid-cols-3\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",\\\"fa9ad58d-a3b5-4047-a09e-4730fbb02e12\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"relative overflow-hidden rounded-sm bg-sandstone-deep/30 col-span-2 aspect-[4/3] sm:col-span-2\\\",\\\"children\\\":[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L22\\\",null,{\\\"src\\\":\\\"https://iiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/tirtha-images/img_13355.jpeg\\\",\\\"alt\\\":\\\"Shri Ranakpura Parshwanath फोटो 1\\\",\\\"fill\\\":true,\\\"sizes\\\":\\\"(min-width: 640px) 33vw, 50vw\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"object-cover\\\"}]}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",\\\"dfb2e241-102c-41e8-a753-95878c481ea9\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"relative overflow-hidden rounded-sm bg-sandstone-deep/30 aspect-square\\\",\\\"children\\\":[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L22\\\",null,{\\\"src\\\":\\\"https://iiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/tirtha-images/img_13316.jpeg\\\",\\\"alt\\\":\\\"Shri Ranakpura Parshwanath फोटो 2\\\",\\\"fill\\\":true,\\\"sizes\\\":\\\"(min-width: 640px) 33vw, 50vw\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"object-cover\\\"}]}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",\\\"b21b0897-2948-4251-a533-82e2ca797e49\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"relative overflow-hidden rounded-sm bg-sandstone-deep/30 aspect-square\\\",\\\"children\\\":[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L22\\\",null,{\\\"src\\\":\\\"https://iiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/tirtha-images/img_13342.jpeg\\\",\\\"alt\\\":\\\"Shri Ranakpura Parshwanath फोटो 3\\\",\\\"fill\\\":true,\\\"sizes\\\":\\\"(min-width: 640px) 33vw, 50vw\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"object-cover\\\"}]}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",\\\"ddbcbfe9-51cf-44f4-9121-93f6a1309c39\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"relative overflow-hidden rounded-sm bg-sandstone-deep/30 aspect-square\\\",\\\"children\\\":[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L22\\\",null,{\\\"src\\\":\\\"https://iiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/tirtha-images/img_13322.jpeg\\\",\\\"alt\\\":\\\"Shri Ranakpura Parshwanath फोटो 4\\\",\\\"fill\\\":true,\\\"sizes\\\":\\\"(min-width: 640px) 33vw, 50vw\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"object-cover\\\"}]}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"div\\\",\\\"82480dcf-313e-410c-85de-6c8654b5f4ce\\\",{\\\"className\\\":\\\"relative overflow-hidden rounded-sm bg-sandstone-deep/30 aspect-square\\\",\\\"children\\\":[\\\"$\\\",\\\"$L22\\\",null,{\\\"src\\\":\\\"https://iiudzezqkqtqcxbkssdq.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/tirtha-images/img_13348.jpeg\\\",\\\"alt\\\":\\\"Shri Ranakpura Parshwanath फोटो 5\\\",\\\"fill\\\":true,\\\"sizes\\\":\\\"(min-width: 640px) 33vw, 50vw\\\",\\\"className\\\":\\\"object-cover\\\"}]}]]}]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"23:T714,\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"History: In the 15th century of the Vikram era, Acharya Shri Somsundarsuriji, a man of magnetic personality, came into contact with the merchant Dharnashah, in whose heart he kindled a powerful spiritual light. At the age of twenty-one, Dharnashah undertook the pilgrimage to Shatrunjay, the holiest of Jain pilgrimage sites. Dharnashah took a vow of lifelong celibacy together with his wife. Owing to his sharp intellect, deep administrative ability, and leadership qualities, he rose to the position of minister under King Rana Kumbha. One day Dharnashah felt a strong urge to build a temple to the first Tirthankara, Lord Shri Rishabhdev (Adinath), one that would have no equal in beauty among other temples. One night, in a dream, Dharnashah beheld the \\\"Nalini Gulm Vimana\\\" (a celestial abode in the twelfth Devalok), considered the most beautiful of the heavenly dwellings of the gods. Dharnashah resolved that the temple should be as beautiful as this heavenly abode. He therefore bought land from Rana Kumbha and founded a village named \\\"Ranpur,\\\" later called Ranakpur. Craftsmen were summoned from near and far and asked to build the temple according to the dream. A beautiful and magnificent three-storey, four-faced (chaturmukh) temple took shape, its carving unique and unmatched in the world. The foundation was laid in the year 1433 of the Vikram era. The entire temple rests on pillars, numbering 1,444 in all, each carved differently. The temple is built so that the Lord's face can be seen from any corner, and not a single pillar obstructs the view. This temple contains 84 images (symbolising liberation from the cycle of 8.4 million forms of rebirth). It is also known by names such as \\\"Dharan Vihar,\\\" \\\"Trilokya Deepak Prasad,\\\" \\\"Tribhuvan Vihar,\\\" \\\"Nalini Gulm Vimana,\\\" and \\\"Chaturmukh Prasad.\\\"\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1d:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"article\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"prose-none space-y-4 font-body text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.85] text-ink\\\",\\\"children\\\":[[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"0\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"108 Parshwanath, No. 79 – Shri Ranakpura Parshwanath\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"1\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Ranakpur, District – Pali.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"2\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Main Deity: A dark-hued image of Lord Shri Ranakpura Parshwanath in the padmasana posture, 19 inches tall and 15 inches wide, tall, beautiful, and set within an exquisitely and artistically carved parikar. Above the head of the image is a canopy of 7 serpent hoods. This parikar is a beautiful example of the carved artistry of its sculptors.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"3\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Location: The unmatched grandeur of the Ranakpur temple stands amid the Aravalli hills, in the heart of a remote and wondrous valley, encircled by forest like a garland around this enchanting landscape.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"4\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"$23\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"5\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Directly opposite the main temple, to the north, stands the temple of Lord Shri Ranakpura Parshwanath, built in the 15th century. It is said that while work was underway on the main temple, the craftsmen, in their spare time, built this temple from materials left over from the main construction, and installed in it the image of Parshwanath, which came to be known as \\\\\\\"Ranakpura Parshwanath.\\\\\\\" Every year a fair (mela) is held here on Kartik Purnima, Falgun Purnima, and Chaitra Purnima.\\\"}],[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"6\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Other Temples: The main temple is that of Lord Adinath. There were 6 other temples here, but today only 3 remain — the other 2 belong to Lord Neminath and Lord Parshwanath, along with a Surya (Sun) temple and a temple of Chakreshwar Devi.\\\"}],\\\"$L24\\\",\\\"$L25\\\",\\\"$L26\\\",\\\"$L27\\\",\\\"$L28\\\",\\\"$L29\\\",\\\"$L2a\\\"]}]\\n\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"1e:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"nav\\\",null,{\\\"className\\\":\\\"mt-14 flex items-center justify-between border-t 