 is built on a high plateau, its three-storey roof of marble, on which the sculptor, through his supreme artistic genius, has carved a poem of devotion and spiritual grandeur in stone. Above the steps of the main entrance (the western gateway) is a magnificent carving of the leaves of the wish-fulfilling Kalpavriksha tree, which fulfils the wishes of pilgrims. On the right can be seen the image of the master mason Drepa (Deva), a native of Mandar, who was a yogi and lived in the temple in the garb of an ascetic; his descendants still live there today and carry out the temple's repair work. Inside the temple, on the left, a statue of Dharnashah has been carved into a pillar so that his name would not be openly advertised, while allowing him to always gaze upon the Lord. There stands a Rayan tree planted by Dharnashah 500 years ago, which still exists today; beneath it are images of the footprints (charan paduka) of Lord Rishabhdev, a reminder that he attained omniscience (kevalgyan) beneath a Rayan tree. At the centre, to the right of the temple, is a marble samavasarana of Lord Mahavir. The scene of the ordeal (upsarg) inflicted upon Lord Parshwanath by Kamath is depicted, with an artistic carving of a canopy of a thousand serpent hoods above the Lord's head, in which sixteen serpent-queens assist a serpent-king in protecting the Lord, their intertwined tails carved with great artistry — this carving is made from a single stone. A model of Shatrunjay has also been well designed here. The image of Lord Parshwanath is made of touchstone. There are two pairs of metal bells weighing 13 mann, whose sound carries up to three miles.\"])</script><script>self.__next_f.push([1,\"24:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"7\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"$2b\\\"}]\\n25:[\\\"$\\\",\\\"p\\\",\\\"8\\\",{\\\"children\\\":\\\"Directions: The nearest railway station, Falna, is 35 km away; the nearest town, Sadri, is 10 km away. Bus service and private vehicles are available. The nearest airport is Udai